<TopicPage slug="subjects/architecture/intermediate/what-buildings-must-do">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Every building, from a hut to a hospital, answers the same brief: <strong>stand up, serve
    its purpose, and be worth inhabiting</strong> — Vitruvius\'s firmitas, utilitas, venustas,
    2,000 years old and still the profession\'s checklist. Our century added a fourth: do it
    without wrecking the climate.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the three classical legs plus the modern fourth" widget="equationStep" config={vitruvCfg} />

  <h2>How architects actually work</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The brief (programme) is negotiated:</strong> clients say what they want; architects discover what they need — the interview is design work (the design lesson\'s UX research, with rooms).</li>
    <li><strong>Sites constrain and inspire:</strong> climate, orientation, neighbours, codes, history. The best buildings answer their site specifically — which is why transplanting a design rarely works.</li>
    <li><strong>Codes are the floor, not the ceiling:</strong> building regulations (fire, structure, accessibility, energy) set minimums; architecture happens above them (the law lesson\'s standards, spatially enforced).</li>
    <li><strong>The unbuilt cost of bad buildings:</strong> badly lit schools, stuffy offices, windy plazas — space shapes behaviour measurably (the environmental-design lesson\'s evidence); architecture is applied psychology with a roof.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Vitruvius, De architectura (c. 25 BCE); RIBA plan-of-work; the statics and design lessons of this atlas.</p>
</TopicPage>
